            {
              "id": "glog-7c637e59-73be-4478-9bb8-84a099cfa3c7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strncpy` function in C++ is a common source of vulnerabilities due to its potential to cause buffer overflow errors. This function copies a specified number of characters from the source string to the destination string. If the source string is longer than the specified number of characters, the function does not append a null character to the destination string, which can lead to buffer overflow.\n\nIn the provided code snippet `strncpy(dst, p, strlen(p))`, the length of the source string `p` is used as the limit for the copy operation. If `p` does not contain a null character within this length, `strncpy` will not append a null character to `dst`, potentially leading to buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer string handling functions that ensure null-termination, such as `strlcpy` or `strncpy_s`. If `strncpy` must be used, always ensure that the destination string is null-terminated manually.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the code using `strlcpy`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string.h>\n\n// ...\n\nchar dst[BUFFER_SIZE];\nstrlcpy(dst, p, sizeof(dst));\n```\n\nIf `strlcpy` is not available, you can use `strncpy` and manually null-terminate the destination string:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string.h>\n\n// ...\n\nchar dst[BUFFER_SIZE];\nstrncpy(dst, p, sizeof(dst) - 1);\ndst[sizeof(dst) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<string.h>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
