              "id": "glog-c24ef453-09ee-43ff-aa52-c32cebc4b0eb",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcat` function in C++ is used to concatenate strings. However, it does not perform any bounds checking, and thus can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities. This is because `strcat` will keep appending data until it hits a null byte (`\\0`), potentially writing past the end of the allocated destination buffer. This can lead to data corruption, crashes, and code execution vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using `strcat` and instead use safer string handling functions that perform bounds checking, such as `strncat`. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcat` function with `strncat` function. Here is how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\nstrncat(argsInfo->createArgs, argv[i], sizeof(argsInfo->createArgs) - strlen(argsInfo->createArgs) - 1);\n```\n\nThis will ensure that no more characters are appended than the destination buffer can hold.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `cstring` or `string.h` for the `strcat` function\n- `cstring` or `string.h` for the `strncat` function\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
