            {
              "id": "glog-61ca5136-59d1-4bd4-ba17-29e6d7a21061",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen()` function in C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to a vulnerability if the string is not null-terminated. This is because `strlen()` counts characters until it reaches a null character. If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen()` will continue reading memory beyond the intended boundary, potentially leading to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behaviors.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. This can be done by initializing your strings with a null character (`'\\0'`) at the end, or by using functions that automatically null-terminate strings, such as `strncpy()`.\n\nAdditionally, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen()`, such as `strnlen()`, which takes a maximum length parameter and will not read beyond this length, even if it does not encounter a null character.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf `desc` is a character array, ensure it is null-terminated:\n\n```cpp\nchar desc[] = \"description\\0\";\nsize_t len = strlen(desc);\n```\n\nIf `desc` is a pointer to a character string, ensure the string it points to is null-terminated:\n\n```cpp\nchar* desc = new char[50];\nstrncpy(desc, \"description\", 49);\ndesc[49] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\nsize_t len = strlen(desc);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen()` function is part of the C Standard Library, so you need to include the `cstring` header:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-d1a9a55a-3ddc-43dc-93d9-70e6e13cab26",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can lead to arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure vulnerabilities.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strcpy(argsInfo.wconfig, \"create.config\")`, the string \"create.config\" is copied into `argsInfo.wconfig` without checking if `argsInfo.wconfig` has enough space to hold the string.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string copy functions that include a length parameter. This allows the function to know the size of the destination buffer and prevent buffer overflows. Functions like `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy` can be used instead.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with `strncpy`:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(argsInfo.wconfig, \"create.config\", sizeof(argsInfo.wconfig) - 1);\nargsInfo.wconfig[sizeof(argsInfo.wconfig) - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n```\n\nThis code will copy the string \"create.config\" into `argsInfo.wconfig`, but it will not exceed the size of `argsInfo.wconfig`. The `- 1` is to leave space for the null terminator.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-c208c4b1-f980-455f-8857-bfaa0cbc2291",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcat` function in C++ is used to concatenate strings. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not perform bounds checking, and thus can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities. This can potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code or cause a denial of service.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strcat(pConfig->rocm_config.rocm_usescases, \"dkms\")`, the `strcat` function is used to append the string \"dkms\" to the `rocm_usescases` string in the `rocm_config` structure of `pConfig`. If the `rocm_usescases` string does not have enough space to accommodate the additional characters, a buffer overflow can occur.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string handling functions that perform bounds checking, such as `strncat` or `strlcat`. These functions take an additional parameter that specifies the maximum number of characters to be appended, thus preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcat` function with `strncat` as follows:\n\n```cpp\nstrncat(pConfig->rocm_config.rocm_usescases, \"dkms\", sizeof(pConfig->rocm_config.rocm_usescases) - strlen(pConfig->rocm_config.rocm_usescases) - 1);\n```\n\nThis code will append at most `sizeof(pConfig->rocm_config.rocm_usescases) - strlen(pConfig->rocm_config.rocm_usescases) - 1` characters from \"dkms\" to `rocm_usescases`, thus preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library for the `strcat` and `strncat` functions, and the `<cstdio>` library for the `sizeof` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS141863"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-16f9f2ab-dac1-49ca-804c-f7ce83552219",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C and C++ is used to determine the length of a null-terminated string. However, if the input to `strlen` is not properly validated, it can lead to vulnerabilities such as buffer overflows or reading from uninitialized memory. This can occur if `strlen` is called on a pointer that does not point to a valid null-terminated string, potentially causing the program to read beyond the intended memory bounds.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Input Validation**: Ensure that all input strings are properly validated and null-terminated before passing them to `strlen`.\n2. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Consider using safer string handling functions or libraries that provide bounds checking, such as `strnlen` or C++ standard library classes like `std::string`.\n3. **Memory Management**: Properly manage memory to ensure that strings are correctly allocated and null-terminated.\n4. **Static Analysis**: Use static analysis tools to detect potential vulnerabilities related to string handling.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <cstring> // Required for strlen\n#include <string>  // Required for std::string\n\nvoid safe_strlen(const char* value) {\n    if (value == nullptr) {\n        std::cerr << \"Error: Null pointer passed to strlen.\" << std::endl;\n        return;\n    }\n\n    // Use std::string to ensure null-termination\n    std::string safe_value(value);\n    size_t length = safe_value.length();\n    std::cout << \"Length of the string is: \" << length << std::endl;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    const char* value = \"Hello, World!\";\n    safe_strlen(value);\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\n- `<iostream>`: For input and output operations.\n- `<cstring>`: For C-style string handling functions like `strlen`.\n- `<string>`: For using `std::string` which provides safer string handling.\n\n### OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-d58b8dc3-806c-4378-bb36-581e55ff444e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen()` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`'\\0'`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen()` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, potentially leading to a buffer overflow or other memory corruption issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. If you're dealing with user input or other untrusted data, make sure to validate and sanitize it before passing it to `strlen()`. Also, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen()`, such as `strnlen()`, which takes a maximum length parameter and won't read past it.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf `pConfig->rocm_config.rocm_usescases` is a string, ensure it is null-terminated before passing it to `strlen()`. If it's not guaranteed to be null-terminated, use `strnlen()` instead:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t len = strnlen(pConfig->rocm_config.rocm_usescases, MAX_LENGTH);\n```\n\nReplace `MAX_LENGTH` with the maximum expected length of the string.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-7be03b1b-e850-4a15-90d7-e21e3b309daa",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C and C++ is used to determine the length of a null-terminated string. However, if the input to `strlen` is not properly validated, it can lead to vulnerabilities such as buffer overflows or reading from uninitialized memory. This can occur if `strlen` is called on a pointer that does not point to a valid null-terminated string, potentially causing the program to read beyond the intended memory bounds.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Input Validation**: Ensure that all input strings are properly validated and null-terminated before passing them to `strlen`.\n2. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Consider using safer string handling functions or libraries that provide bounds checking, such as `strnlen` or C++ standard library classes like `std::string`.\n3. **Memory Management**: Properly manage memory to ensure that strings are correctly allocated and null-terminated.\n4. **Static Analysis**: Use static analysis tools to detect potential vulnerabilities related to string handling.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <cstring> // Required for strlen\n#include <string>  // Required for std::string\n\nvoid safe_strlen(const char* value) {\n    if (value == nullptr) {\n        std::cerr << \"Error: Null pointer passed to strlen.\" << std::endl;\n        return;\n    }\n\n    // Use std::string to ensure null-termination\n    std::string safe_value(value);\n    size_t length = safe_value.length();\n    std::cout << \"Length of the string is: \" << length << std::endl;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    const char* value = \"Hello, World!\";\n    safe_strlen(value);\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\n- `<iostream>`: For input and output operations.\n- `<cstring>`: For C-style string handling functions like `strlen`.\n- `<string>`: For using `std::string` which provides safer string handling.\n\n### OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)"
