            {
              "id": "glog-c53b34d4-ce15-4caa-ba31-f30c9d6237fd",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcat` function in C++ is a standard library function that concatenates strings. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not perform bounds checking, and thus can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities. In the provided code snippet, `strcat` is used to append a string to `pConfig->installer_name_with_extension`, which can potentially lead to a buffer overflow if the size of the string exceeds the size of the buffer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer string handling functions that perform bounds checking, such as `strncat`, `strlcat`, or `snprintf`. These functions take an additional parameter that specifies the maximum number of characters to be appended, thus preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcat` function with `strncat`:\n\n```cpp\nstrncat(pConfig->installer_name_with_extension, \".run\", sizeof(pConfig->installer_name_with_extension) - strlen(pConfig->installer_name_with_extension) - 1);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the provided links are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
