            {
              "id": "glog-7a9dfacd-2412-4a91-8feb-d860a7e57236",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can lead to arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string copy functions that check the size of the destination buffer. These include `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `snprintf`. These functions take an additional parameter that specifies the size of the destination buffer, preventing buffer overflow vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with a safer function, such as `strncpy`. Here is how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(copy, pConfig->rocm_usescases, sizeof(copy));\ncopy[sizeof(copy) - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null termination\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `cstring` or `string.h` for the `strcpy` or `strncpy` function\n- `config.h` for the `pConfig` object (assuming it is defined there)\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
