              "id": "glog-eb85cb17-44d0-4c34-9046-c40a1f1c0c14",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strncat` function in C++ is a standard library function that concatenates strings. However, it is considered unsafe and its use is generally discouraged because it can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities. The function does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to writing beyond the allocated space if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can corrupt data, crash the program, or allow the execution of malicious code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string handling functions that check the size of the destination buffer. In C++, the `std::string` class and its associated functions are generally safer and more convenient to use than C-style strings and functions. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strncat` function with the `strncat_s` function, which is a safer alternative. The `strncat_s` function checks the size of the destination buffer and ensures that it does not write beyond the allocated space.\n\nHere is how you can fix the code:\n\n```cpp\n// Assuming query is a char array and c is a char variable\nsize_t len = strlen(query);\nsize_t remaining = sizeof(query) - len - 1;\nstrncat_s(query, remaining, &c, 1);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library for the `strncat` function and the `<cstddef>` library for the `size_t` type.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
